Found by a coordinator feature-gap review, 2026-08-30. Enhancement.

## Gap
The server declares no `color_provider`, so `textDocument/documentColor` and
`textDocument/colorPresentation` are unimplemented. Openplanet plugin code is
saturated with colors in two distinct forms:

1. **Numeric colors** — `vec3(1, 0, 0)` and `vec4(r, g, b, a)` passed to
   UI/Draw calls. `vec3`/`vec4` are already first-class in the type checker
   (they appear across src/typecheck/), so the types are known.
2. **Openplanet color escape codes in strings** — the `$xxx` form (e.g.
   `"\\$f00red text"`), used throughout UI strings and log output. These are
   invisible in the editor today; you have to know the hex by heart.

Neither gets a swatch, and there is no picker.

## Why this one is worth building
It is the most domain-specific win available. Every other gap on the list is
"catch up to what other language servers do"; this one is a feature no generic
AngelScript tooling will ever have, because the `$xxx` escape is Openplanet's
own and the vec3/vec4-as-color convention is this ecosystem's. It is also
cheap: the analysis surface (typed expressions, string literals with spans)
already exists.

## Work
- Advertise `color_provider` and implement `textDocument/documentColor`:
  - `vec3(...)`/`vec4(...)` constructor calls with literal numeric arguments.
    Report in 0..1 space, which is what the game uses.
  - `$xxx` escapes inside string literals (3 hex digits, Openplanet form).
    Confirm the exact grammar from Openplanet docs before implementing —
    check whether `$xxxxxx`, named codes, or `$z`/`$o`/`$g`-style control
    codes exist, and handle only true colors.
- Implement `textDocument/colorPresentation` so the picker writes back in the
  SAME form it read: a `$f00` stays a `$f00`, a `vec3` stays a `vec3` and does
  not become a `vec4`. Round-tripping the user's chosen representation is the
  whole usability point.
- Decide precision for the numeric form: a picker returning 0.5019607 for
  `128/255` is worse than useless in source. Recommend rounding to 3-4
  significant figures and preserving the literal style already in the file
  (int-looking `1` vs `1.0` vs `1.0f`).

## Design decision (coordinator, 2026-08-30)
Only literal-argument constructors get a swatch. `vec3(x, y, z)` with variable
arguments must NOT be reported — a swatch that cannot be edited is noise, and
guessing a runtime value is wrong. Same for computed string concatenation: only
`$xxx` inside a literal is reported.

Do not report `vec3` used as a POSITION or direction. There is no way to
distinguish that from a color at the type level, so scope the swatch to vec3/
vec4 in ARGUMENT position of a call whose parameter is known to be a color, if
the typedb makes that determinable; otherwise report all literal vec3/vec4 and
accept the false positives, but say which choice was made and why. Prefer the
narrower rule if the information is available.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)
Pure-function tests over the color extraction, no server needed.

Red:
- `document_color_finds_vec3_literal`: `vec3(1, 0, 0)` -> one color, red,
  range covering the constructor call.
- `document_color_finds_dollar_escape_in_string`: `"\\$f00hi"` -> one color,
  red, range covering exactly the `$f00`, not the whole string.
- `color_presentation_round_trips_form`: editing a `$f00` yields a `$`-form
  presentation; editing a `vec3` yields a `vec3` presentation.

Variants (per CLAUDE.md, generalize):
- `vec3` and `vec4` both handled; alpha preserved on vec4 round-trip
- literal styles `1`, `1.0`, `1.0f`, `1.f`, `.5` all parse; the presentation
  preserves the style already used in that call
- out-of-range values (`vec3(2, -1, 0)`) -> clamped for DISPLAY, and the write
  back does not silently clamp the user's source
- non-literal args (`vec3(x, 0, 0)`) -> NO color reported
- nested calls (`vec3(f(1), 0, 0)`) -> no color
- multiple colors on one line -> all reported with correct, non-overlapping
  ranges
- `$xxx` variants: lowercase, uppercase, mixed hex; an invalid `$zzz` -> not
  reported; `$$` escape (if it means a literal dollar) -> not reported
- several `$xxx` in one string -> each gets its own range
- `$xxx` at the very start and very end of a string literal
- multi-byte characters before a `$xxx` in the same string -> range is correct
  in UTF-16 code units (LSP ranges are UTF-16; getting this wrong shifts the
  swatch, and this repo already has display-width handling in the TUI to
  contrast with)
- a string containing `$xxx` inside a COMMENT -> decide and test (recommend
  reported, comments are where example strings live)

## Related
Extension needs no change — VS Code renders color decorators natively once the
server advertises the capability.

